---
/**
 * About.
 *
 * ProfilePage, because that is what it is: the page that identifies the Person
 * the rest of the site's structured data references. It is also the page most
 * likely to be quoted when something summarises who Bryce is, so the first
 * paragraph answers that question outright rather than building to it.
 *
 * EVERY CLAIM HERE IS CORROBORATED. Facts that appeared in only one place, or
 * that sources disagreed on, were left out rather than hedged. AGENTS.md
 * forbids fabricating detail about a real person, and a bio is exactly where
 * that temptation is strongest.
 *
 * Specifically still absent, and deliberately: the size and timing of the
 * first raise, and which Washington town the company is based in. Both are
 * widely repeated online and the sources disagree, so neither is on the page.
 * They are not missing because nobody thought of them — if either is ever
 * added it should come from Bryce, not from a search result.
 *
 * This used to be stated in a COLOPHON panel on the page itself. It was
 * removed at his request; the reasoning stays here, where it is aimed at
 * whoever edits this file next rather than at a reader.
 */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';

import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import portrait from '../assets/bryce-decora.png';
import { person } from '../data/person';
---

<PageLayout
  seo={{
    title: 'About Bryce DeCora',
    description:
      'Co-founder and CEO of CloseBot. Former Boeing engineer who left to teach himself to code, and now builds AI that handles sales conversations.',
    openGraph: { type: 'profile' },
  }}
  pageType="ProfilePage"
  heading="About"
  lede="Co-founder and CEO of CloseBot. Former Boeing engineer, self-taught developer, and now someone whose software talks to a lot of strangers."
  width="wide"
>
  <div class="lg:grid lg:grid-cols-[14rem_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-x-16">
    <div class="mb-10 lg:mb-0">
      {/*
        Framed as a print. The source photograph has a white studio
        background, which would read as a rendering mistake sitting on cream —
        the hairline and hard offset shadow make that white deliberate, so it
        looks like a photograph on the page rather than a transparency that
        failed. Displayed at 176px against a 332px source, so it stays sharp
        on a 2x display.
      */}
      <Image
        src={portrait}
        alt="Bryce DeCora"
        width={176}
        height={176}
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
        class="border border-line shadow-[3px_3px_0_var(--color-ink)]"
      />

      <dl class="mt-8 space-y-4">
        <div>
          <dt class="kicker">Role</dt>
          <dd class="text-small mt-1 text-ink">{person.jobTitle}</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="kicker">Company</dt>
          <dd class="text-small mt-1">
            <a
              href={person.worksFor.url}
              rel="noopener"
              class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-accent-hover"
            >
              {person.worksFor.name}
            </a>
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="kicker">Email</dt>
          <dd class="text-small mt-1">
            <a
              href={`mailto:${person.email}`}
              class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-accent-hover"
            >
              {person.email}
            </a>
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I co-founded <a href={person.worksFor.url} rel="noopener">CloseBot</a>, an
          AI platform that qualifies leads and books appointments by having an
          actual conversation with them. It runs across the channels businesses
          already use, and it works at three in the morning, which is the part
          that turns out to matter most.
        </p>

        <h2>How I got here</h2>

        <p>
          I trained as a mechanical engineer at the South Dakota School of Mines
          and Technology, then spent several years at Boeing as an equipment and
          tool engineer, a production tooling stress engineer, and an automation
          control engineer. It was good work. It was also work where the thing I
          built and the thing a customer felt were separated by several years and
          a lot of other people.
        </p>

        <p>
          {/*
            Cobras.io and Finite Languages are named but NOT linked: cobras.io
            no longer resolves. A dead link on a bio page is worse than no link,
            and it is the kind of thing nobody notices for a year.
          */}
          So I left, and taught myself to write software. Before that I had built
          Cobras.io and Finite Languages — the first to manage client
          communications, the second a platform for multilingual bloggers. Both
          taught me more about what people will actually put up with in a
          product than any amount of planning would have.
        </p>

        <h2>Why CloseBot exists</h2>

        <p>
          The problem is response latency. A lead that gets a real reply in
          minutes converts at a dramatically higher rate than one that gets a
          reply the next morning, and no human team covers every hour. I had
          built a rough version of an AI qualifier for my own business before
          ChatGPT launched. When it launched, it was obvious what the rough
          version could become, and I went all in.
        </p>

        <p>
          CloseBot has since booked over a million appointments and handles six
          figures' worth of messages a day. The number I actually care about is
          different: how often the person on the other end could not tell, and
          did not need to.
        </p>

        <h2>What I write about here</h2>

        <p>
          Mostly what I have learned operating an AI setter at scale: what the
          published benchmarks get wrong, where these systems genuinely fail,
          and the decisions behind how we build them. Some of it is
          uncomfortable for a vendor to publish, which is roughly how I pick
          what to write about.
        </p>

        <h2>Getting in touch</h2>

        {/*
          The profile list is built from `person.socialProfiles` — the same
          array that produces the header, the footer, and schema.org `sameAs`.
          It was a hard-coded LinkedIn URL, which is exactly the duplication
          AGENTS.md means by "no duplicated business data": adding Instagram
          and Facebook to the entity would have left this sentence quietly
          claiming LinkedIn was the only one.
        */}
        <p>
          Email is best: <a href={`mailto:${person.email}`}>{person.email}</a>.
          I am also on {
            person.socialProfiles.map((profile, i) => (
              <Fragment>
                {i > 0 && (i === person.socialProfiles.length - 1 ? ' and ' : ', ')}
                <a href={profile.url} rel="me noopener">{profile.label}</a>
              </Fragment>
            ))
          }.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
